@startuml module-coupling

' What actually calls what, at the module level — not a re-drawing of the
' entity-relationship diagram's foreign keys with a boundary fence around
' them. A foreign key (order_items.product_id -> products.id) means one
' row references another; it does not mean the Order module "depends on"
' Catalogue the way a PHP `use` statement does. Coding-conventions.md's
' "no repository pattern, Eloquent is the repository" rule means a read
' crossing an area boundary through Eloquent is deliberate, not a coupling
' violation — this diagram is about the other kind of coupling: one
' Action's code calling another area's Action or Support class.
'
' Modules are app/Actions/{Area}/ folders, the same ten areas
' reference/schema/schema.md groups tables into (Cart, Catalogue, Contact,
' Content, Coupon, Inventory, Order, Payment, ProductReview, Shipment),
' plus App\Support as an eleventh module for the calculators, resolvers,
' and value objects several areas share (Money, the Resolve* classes,
' CalculateCartTotals/CalculateCouponDiscount/CalculateDeliveryPrice).
'
' Every edge below is a real `use` statement, found by grepping every
' Action file's imports for another area's App\Actions\{Area}\ or
' App\Support\{Class}, not inferred from the schema. Two things this
' diagram deliberately does NOT show, because they are not this kind of
' coupling: the presentation layer calling into an Action (every module
' is called from Filament/Livewire/Http — that is what modules are FOR,
' not a coupling problem between them), and an Action or Model reading a
' relation on another area's Eloquent model directly.
'
' The result is the actual argument for low coupling: 10 modules, 6
' cross-module Action-to-Action edges, all one-directional, no cycles.
' Verified against the codebase on 2026-09-07.

title Action-to-Action module coupling — 10 modules, 6 cross-module edges, no cycles

skinparam packageStyle rectangle

package "Cart" as Cart
package "Catalogue" as Catalogue
package "Contact" as Contact
package "Content" as Content
package "Coupon" as Coupon
package "Inventory" as Inventory
package "Order" as Order
package "Payment" as Payment
package "ProductReview" as ProductReview
package "Shipment" as Shipment
package "Support\n(Money, Resolve*, Calculate*)" as Support

Catalogue --> Inventory : AddProductVariation\n-> RecordInventoryMovement
note right on link
  A new variation starts an
  inventory row: creating the
  variation IS the initial stock
  movement (ADR-0002).
end note

Order --> Inventory : TransitionOrderStatus\n-> CompleteSale / ReleaseStock / RestockReturn
note right on link
  The one place ADR-0011's
  inventory side effects live —
  a status change moves stock,
  never the reverse.
end note

Order --> Coupon : CreateOrder -> RedeemCoupon
note right on link
  Redemption is recorded at
  order creation, not at
  checkout-preview time.
end note

Order ..> Support : CreateOrder ->\nMoney, ResolveVariationPrice,\nCalculateDeliveryPrice, CalculateCouponDiscount,\nCouponDiscountLine
Coupon ..> Support : ApplyCoupon / RedeemCoupon ->\nCalculateCartTotals, CalculateCouponDiscount,\nCouponDiscountLine
Catalogue ..> Support : CreateProduct / UpdateProduct /\nSetProductAttributeValues / UpdateProductCategory ->\nResolveAllowedAttributes, ResolveCategoryFamily
Payment ..> Support : every Payment Action ->\nMoney

note bottom
  Not drawn — deliberately: Cart, Contact, Content, ProductReview and
  Shipment have zero outgoing edges to another Actions area. Each is
  called from the presentation layer (Filament/Livewire/Http) but never
  calls into a sibling module's Actions — the actual "high cohesion" half
  of the claim this diagram makes.
end note

@enduml
